// Package tlsca gives the management console a TLS certificate that the
// application makes for itself.
//
// The design: **HTTP for the guest portal, HTTPS for the management console**, the
// certificate is self-generated, and **every parameter is generated by the
// application** — the operator types nothing. No prompt, no flag
// to discover, no openssl invocation in a README. The binary starts, finds no
// certificate, makes one, and serves.
//
// The lifetime is 825 days rather than ten years, because ten years is a console
// macOS refuses — measured, below — and because the requirement that matters is the
// one about the operator typing nothing: a console the operator's own Mac will not
// open is a thing the operator has to handle. Renewal is what pays for the shorter
// lifetime, and the two arrived together.
//
// The portal keeps HTTP for a measured reason that is not laziness: a self-signed
// certificate in front of a captive portal is an interstitial the Captive Network
// Assistant cannot click through. The console has an operator with a mouse, which
// is exactly the audience an authority warning is survivable for. That asymmetry is
// the whole of why this package exists for one listener and not for both.
//
// # 825 days, because that is what Apple accepts — measured
//
// Ten years is the obvious default, and the reasoning behind it is the 398-day
// exemption for locally-operated CAs. Two citations support that exemption and both
// are quoted correctly:
//
// Chromium, net/docs/certificate_lifetimes.md, on ERR_CERT_VALIDITY_TOO_LONG:
//
//	"This will only apply to TLS server certificates from CAs that are trusted in
//	a default installation of Google Chrome, commonly known as 'publicly trusted
//	CAs' and will not apply to locally-operated CAs that have been manually
//	configured."
//
// Apple, "About upcoming limits on trusted certificates"
// (support.apple.com/en-us/102028, formerly HT211025), on the 398-day rule:
//
//	"This change will not affect certificates issued from user-added or
//	administrator-added Root CAs."
//
// **Both are true, both are about a different rule, and together they suggest a
// false conclusion.** Apple's *earlier* requirement — HT210176, 825 days or fewer —
// carries no such exemption, and it is the one that binds. Measured on macOS with
// Apple's own evaluator and the certificate supplied as its own anchor:
// 825 days verifies, **826 days is CSSMERR_TP_CERT_SUSPENDED**, and so is 3 650.
//
// Read the `-r` in that measurement carefully, because it is the part that decides
// the design: the certificate was supplied as its **own trust anchor**. Trusting it
// is not a way around the limit. Ten years is a console the operator's own Mac
// refuses with no click-through and no operator action that recovers it.
//
// So this package issues at exactly 825 days: the most the strictest evaluator
// accepts. Issuing less buys nothing and costs the operator a re-trust sooner.
//
// TestUntrustedFailsOnAuthorityNotOnValidity still pins what Go can observe — an
// untrusted certificate fails on x509.UnknownAuthorityError, so the warning an
// operator clicks through is about authority. Go applies no lifetime ceiling of its
// own, which is why that test cannot catch the Apple limit and why the
// measurement is made on a Mac.
//
// # Never overwrite — scoped to what it was actually protecting
//
// The rule exists so that an operator who has accepted a fingerprint is not
// silently un-trusted by a restart. **An expired certificate protects nobody**: it
// is a console nobody can reach, and refusing to replace it is loyalty to a file
// rather than to the person the rule is for.
//
// So the rule is: never overwrite a certificate that is still **valid**.
//
//   - Absent → generate.
//   - Present and comfortably valid → load, touch nothing.
//   - Present and expired, or within RenewBeforeDays of expiry → **replace**, and
//     say so through Describe, because the fingerprint the operator wrote down has
//     just changed.
//   - Present but unloadable → refuse. An unreadable file is not *known* to be
//     expired; it may be a wrong mount with the real certificate elsewhere, and
//     destroying it destroys the evidence.
//   - One of the two present → refuse. The survivor may be the valid half.
//
// For the first two the guarantee is structural rather than checked: files are
// created with O_EXCL, so the kernel refuses a write over an existing path even if
// the stat that preceded it raced. The stat is there for the message, not for the
// promise. Renewal is the one path that writes over an existing file, and it goes
// through staged temporaries and rename so a failure leaves the old pair intact —
// an expired certificate with a loud warning is recoverable, an empty directory on
// a box nobody can reach is not.
//
// # Renewal, and the window
//
// RenewBeforeDays is 60. The argument, since the number is otherwise arbitrary:
//
//   - **EnsureSelfSigned checks at process start and nowhere else.** So for that
//     entry point the window is an assumption about restart cadence: a process
//     restarting at least once every 60 days never serves an expired certificate.
//     **That assumption is false for what deploy/ installs** — there is no timer,
//     no cron entry, no unattended reboot and no periodic pull anywhere in
//     deploy/. Rotating is the answer to that, and it is why the answer
//     is not a larger window: against an unbounded gap every window loses.
//   - **Widening it is nearly free.** The certificate rotates every (825 − window)
//     days, so 60 costs 7% of the lifetime — over a five-year box that is the
//     difference between two and three re-trusts. Doubling the window from 30 to 60
//     doubles the restart gap it tolerates for almost nothing.
//   - **Narrowing it fails softly.** If the window is too small the console breaks
//     until the next restart, which then repairs it. That is bounded and
//     self-healing, which is why 60 rather than something much larger: each
//     rotation costs a human a re-trust, and that is the expensive step here.
//
// **What EnsureSelfSigned alone cannot promise:** a process that runs continuously
// past the renewal point still reaches expiry in service. A one-shot function
// cannot fix that, so Rotating (rotating.go) runs the same check on every
// handshake and is what the caller should serve through.
//
// **Rotating is inert unless the caller adopts it.** A tls.Config carrying
// Certificates rather than GetCertificate never calls it, and nothing in this
// package can tell; without it the guarantee is only "no process *starts* with a
// certificate expiring within 60 days", and 825 days is long enough that the day
// it matters is a day nobody is watching. cmd/foyer serves the console through
// GetCertificate for that reason.
//
// # Key type: ECDSA P-256
//
//   - Ed25519 is refused outright, and this is the decisive constraint rather than
//     a preference: no shipping browser verifies an Ed25519 TLS certificate.
//     Choosing it would produce a console that does not load at all, which is
//     strictly worse than one that warns.
//   - RSA works everywhere too, but deploy/Dockerfile.foyer builds this binary
//     CGO_ENABLED=0 on alpine, so key generation is Go's own pure-Go code with no
//     libcrypto underneath it. RSA-3072 keygen there is seconds to tens of seconds
//     of variance on a low-power box, on the startup path, looking like a hang.
//     P-256 is immediate.
//   - P-256's ~128-bit security is the stronger choice against RSA-2048's ~112,
//     and ECDSA-with-SHA-256 is the most widely verified signature algorithm on the
//     web. The horizon is 825 days plus renewals rather than a decade, which
//     weakens this argument without reversing it.
//
// # Not a CA, deliberately
//
// This is a self-signed **leaf**: BasicConstraints CA:FALSE, keyUsage
// digitalSignature (not keyEncipherment, which an ECDSA key never uses and which
// only widens what the certificate claims), extendedKeyUsage serverAuth.
//
// The alternative shape — a per-install root that signs a leaf, which is what
// deploy/first-boot.sh does for the Policy API — was weighed and not taken.
// It is genuinely better in one respect: the leaf could be reissued when the host's
// address changes without invalidating what the operator installed. It is worse in
// the respect that matters more, because an operator who installs a *root* on their
// laptop has installed something that can mint a certificate for any name at all,
// and an operator who trusts a *leaf* has trusted one certificate for one host.
// PacketFence and authentik both ship the leaf shape for their own consoles;
// authentik's CertificateBuilder emits CA:FALSE for exactly this certificate.
//
// The cost is one real friction and it is not hidden: Firefox's Authorities import
// refuses a certificate that is not a CA. On Firefox the operator uses the per-site
// exception instead, which is permanent and is scoped to this host. macOS Keychain
// and the Windows root store both accept a leaf directly.
//
// # Subject alternative names, and the one they cannot cover
//
// A browser checks the name it dialled and ignores the CommonName entirely, so the
// names are derived rather than asked for: localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, this host's
// own hostname (plus its first label and that label under .local, because Bonjour
// is how a LAN operator reaches a small box), and every non-loopback global-unicast
// address the machine has. Two warnings teach an operator to click through both, so
// a name mismatch on top of the authority warning is worth this much work to avoid.
//
// **What derivation cannot reach, and what replaced the workaround:** inside a
// container, net.InterfaceAddrs reports the container's addresses and not the
// Docker host's. This was written down as survivable because FOYER_ADMIN_ADDR
// defaulted to loopback and the documented path was an SSH tunnel to 127.0.0.1 —
// and the survivability was a property of that default, not of this package. The
// console is published on the host's LAN address, so the address nobody can derive
// is the only address anybody dials, and the edge is not tolerable.
//
// The remedy is a second source of names that is declared rather than derived:
// EnsureSelfSigned takes the names the deployment says this console answers to,
// and deploy/ computes them from the host so no operator types one. A declared
// name the stored certificate does not cover is a replacement, on the same
// argument as an expired one — see needsRenewal. Derived names are deliberately
// NOT part of that rule: they change whenever a container is recreated, and a
// fingerprint that rotates on every `docker compose up` is trust-on-first-use
// spent until it means nothing.
//
// # File modes
//
// The key is 0400 and the certificate 0644, which is what deploy/verify.sh already
// asserts for every private key and every certificate on that host.
//
// The key's mode is enforced on **every** call, generated or loaded — that is
// keep()'s split, and it is how a tightening reaches an install that already
// exists. The certificate's mode is set at creation only. The asymmetry is the
// point: narrowing a key that is group-readable fixes an exposure, and widening a
// certificate somebody deliberately restricted fixes nothing.
//
// # Empty paths mean "generate, do not persist"
//
// With both paths empty the certificate is made in memory and nothing is written,
// so `go run ./cmd/foyer` serves HTTPS with no configuration at all. One path empty
// and the other set is a refusal, the same species as the partial-file case.
//
// A certificate that changes on every restart is the honest signal that nothing was
// persisted — and it is also a thing that must never be mistaken for a deployment,
// which is what Describe exists to prevent.
package tlsca

import (
	"crypto/ecdsa"
	"crypto/elliptic"
	"crypto/rand"
	"crypto/sha256"
	"crypto/tls"
	"crypto/x509"
	"crypto/x509/pkix"
	"encoding/pem"
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"io/fs"
	"math/big"
	"net"
	"os"
	"path/filepath"
	"sort"
	"strings"
	"time"
)

const (
	// MaxValidityDays is the longest validity period Apple's trust evaluator
	// accepts, and therefore exactly the validity this package issues. There is no
	// reason to issue less: a shorter certificate buys nothing here and costs the
	// operator a re-trust sooner.
	//
	// The 825/826 boundary was **measured**, on Apple's own evaluator, with the
	// certificate supplied as its own anchor, as the package doc records.
	MaxValidityDays = 825

	// RenewBeforeDays is how close to expiry a certificate may come before this
	// package replaces it rather than loading it. See "Renewal" in the package
	// doc for the argument, and for the guarantee it can and cannot make.
	RenewBeforeDays = 60

	// keyMode and certMode are deploy/verify.sh's convention, not a new one: every
	// private key on that host is asserted at 400 and every certificate at 644.
	keyMode  fs.FileMode = 0o400
	certMode fs.FileMode = 0o644
	// dirMode matches ${SECRETS} and ${TLS} in deploy/first-boot.sh.
	dirMode fs.FileMode = 0o700

	// backdate absorbs a clock skew between this host and the operator's browser.
	// A certificate that is not valid for another four minutes is refused with a
	// message about the date, which is a far more confusing thing to debug than
	// the authority warning it would be hiding behind.
	backdate = time.Hour
)

// EnsureSelfSigned loads the certificate at certPath/keyPath, or generates one if
// either is absent.
//
// The bool reports whether it generated, so a caller can log it once. It is enough
// to tell the three outcomes apart *because the caller knows what it passed*:
// generated with a path is "written to disk", generated without one is "in memory
// and gone at the next restart", and not generated is "loaded". Describe turns
// those three into the three different sentences they are; a caller that writes its
// own sentence from the bool alone will eventually write the wrong one.
//
// It never overwrites a certificate that is still valid. One that is expired, or
// within RenewBeforeDays of expiry, is replaced — see "Never overwrite" in the
// package doc for why those are the same rule and not an exception to it. A
// certificate present without its key, or a key without its certificate, is an
// error naming both paths.
func EnsureSelfSigned(certPath, keyPath string, required []string) (tls.Certificate, bool, error) {
	// Parsed before anything is read or written, so a name the deployment got
	// wrong is a startup refusal naming the value rather than a console that
	// silently answers on a certificate no browser will accept for it.
	if _, _, err := splitNames(required); err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, false, err
	}

	switch {
	case certPath == "" && keyPath == "":
		// Generate and persist nothing. Development, and the honest signal for it
		// is that the fingerprint changes on every restart.
		cert, err := generate(required, time.Now())
		return cert, err == nil, err
	case certPath == "":
		return tls.Certificate{}, false, fmt.Errorf(
			"tlsca: a key path (%s) was given with no certificate path: set both to persist a certificate, or neither to generate one in memory", keyPath)
	case keyPath == "":
		return tls.Certificate{}, false, fmt.Errorf(
			"tlsca: a certificate path (%s) was given with no key path: set both to persist a certificate, or neither to generate one in memory", certPath)
	}

	certThere, err := isFile(certPath)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, false, err
	}
	keyThere, err := isFile(keyPath)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, false, err
	}

	switch {
	case certThere && keyThere:
		cert, err := load(certPath, keyPath)
		if err != nil {
			return tls.Certificate{}, false, err
		}
		if !needsRenewal(cert.Leaf, required, time.Now()) {
			return cert, false, nil
		}
		// Expired, or close enough that the next restart might not come in time.
		// This is the ONE case that overwrites, and the rule it narrows is stated
		// in the package doc: never-overwrite protects a certificate somebody may
		// have trusted, and an expired certificate protects nobody.
		fresh, err := renew(certPath, keyPath, required, time.Now())
		return fresh, err == nil, err
	case certThere != keyThere:
		// Refuse. Do not regenerate, and do not delete the survivor.
		present, absent := certPath, keyPath
		if keyThere {
			present, absent = keyPath, certPath
		}
		return tls.Certificate{}, false, fmt.Errorf(
			"tlsca: %s exists but %s does not, so this is half a certificate and it will not be regenerated over: "+
				"an operator may have added the surviving certificate to a trust store, and replacing it silently is "+
				"indistinguishable from an attacker having done so. Restore the missing file, or remove %s to generate a new pair",
			present, absent, present)
	}

	cert, err := generateTo(certPath, keyPath, required, time.Now())
	return cert, err == nil, err
}

// Fingerprint is the SHA-256 of the certificate's DER, in the colon-separated
// uppercase hex every browser's certificate viewer shows.
//
// An operator told "the browser will warn once, that is expected" has no way to
// tell this warning from an attacker's. The fingerprint, printed once where the
// certificate was made, is the only thing that closes that gap — the same idea as
// internal/nas's trust-on-first-use pinning, pointed the other way: there the
// fingerprint is what a later connection is checked against, here it is what a
// human checks the first connection against.
//
// It returns "" for a certificate with no DER, so a caller may print it
// unconditionally.
func Fingerprint(cert tls.Certificate) string {
	if len(cert.Certificate) == 0 {
		return ""
	}
	sum := sha256.Sum256(cert.Certificate[0])
	parts := make([]string, len(sum))
	for i, b := range sum {
		parts[i] = fmt.Sprintf("%02X", b)
	}
	return strings.Join(parts, ":")
}

// Describe is the one line to log about the outcome of EnsureSelfSigned.
//
// It exists because "generated and written to <path>" and "generated in memory,
// persisted nothing" are different sentences and only one of them belongs in a
// production log — and the difference is invisible from inside this package, which
// cannot know whether it is in a container or on a laptop. This does not make the
// wrong sentence impossible; it makes it something a caller has to go out of its
// way to write.
//
// Pass exactly what EnsureSelfSigned was given and returned.
func Describe(cert tls.Certificate, certPath string, generated bool) string {
	fp := Fingerprint(cert)
	leaf := cert.Leaf
	if leaf == nil && len(cert.Certificate) > 0 {
		leaf, _ = x509.ParseCertificate(cert.Certificate[0])
	}

	var b strings.Builder
	switch {
	case generated && certPath == "":
		b.WriteString("console TLS: generated a self-signed certificate IN MEMORY and persisted nothing. " +
			"It changes on every restart, so no fingerprint an operator writes down stays true and nothing can be " +
			"added to a trust store. This is the development shape: give this process a certificate path to persist one")
	case generated:
		// One sentence for a first boot and for a replacement, because the return
		// type cannot tell them apart and this package will not guess. The
		// conditional clause is doing real work: on a first boot it is
		// inapplicable and harmless, and on a replacement it is the only warning
		// an operator gets that a fingerprint they already accepted has just
		// changed under them.
		//
		// **The causes are enumerated rather than named.** There are three, and
		// naming the wrong one is worse than naming none: an operator comparing a
		// changed fingerprint against an explanation that does not match their
		// situation learns the opposite of the truth. All three are checkable from
		// the rest of this line — the expiry is printed, and so are the names.
		b.WriteString("console TLS: generated a self-signed certificate and wrote it to " + certPath +
			". If you had already accepted a different fingerprint for this console, it has been REPLACED, and you " +
			"will be asked to trust this one. A stored certificate is replaced for exactly three reasons: it had " +
			"expired or was close to it, it was valid for longer than browsers now accept, or it did not cover a " +
			"name this console is reached by — the expiry and the names below say which")
	default:
		b.WriteString("console TLS: loaded the certificate at " + certPath)
	}

	if fp != "" {
		b.WriteString("; SHA-256 " + fp)
	}
	if leaf != nil {
		b.WriteString("; names " + strings.Join(names(leaf), ", "))
		b.WriteString("; expires " + leaf.NotAfter.UTC().Format(time.DateOnly))
		if time.Now().After(leaf.NotAfter) {
			b.WriteString(" — WHICH HAS PASSED: every browser will refuse this certificate on validity rather than " +
				"warn on authority. Remove both files and restart to generate a new one")
		}
	}
	if generated {
		b.WriteString(". The browser will warn once that this authority is unknown; compare that fingerprint before accepting it")
	}
	return b.String()
}

// names renders a leaf's subjectAltNames the way the fingerprint line wants them.
func names(leaf *x509.Certificate) []string {
	out := make([]string, 0, len(leaf.DNSNames)+len(leaf.IPAddresses))
	out = append(out, leaf.DNSNames...)
	for _, ip := range leaf.IPAddresses {
		out = append(out, ip.String())
	}
	return out
}

// isFile reports whether path is an existing regular file.
//
// A directory at either path is an error rather than "absent": generating into it
// would fail later with a message about the write, and the operator's actual
// mistake was the path.
func isFile(path string) (bool, error) {
	info, err := os.Stat(path)
	switch {
	case err == nil && info.Mode().IsRegular():
		return true, nil
	case err == nil:
		return false, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: %s is not a regular file (%s), so it is neither a certificate to load nor a place to write one", path, info.Mode().Type())
	case errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist):
		return false, nil
	default:
		return false, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: %s: %w", path, err)
	}
}

// load reads an existing pair and enforces the key's mode.
//
// It does not regenerate on any ground — not an expired certificate, not a missing
// SAN, not a key type this package would no longer choose. Every one of those is a
// reason to *tell* the operator (Describe says so for expiry) and none of them is a
// reason to replace a file somebody may have trusted.
func load(certPath, keyPath string) (tls.Certificate, error) {
	if err := enforceKeyMode(keyPath); err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}
	cert, err := tls.LoadX509KeyPair(certPath, keyPath)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf(
			"tlsca: the certificate at %s and the key at %s could not be loaded (%w). They are left exactly as they are: "+
				"remove both to generate a new pair", certPath, keyPath, err)
	}
	if cert.Leaf == nil && len(cert.Certificate) > 0 {
		cert.Leaf, err = x509.ParseCertificate(cert.Certificate[0])
		if err != nil {
			return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: parsing the certificate at %s: %w", certPath, err)
		}
	}
	return cert, nil
}

// enforceKeyMode narrows a private key to keyMode, on every call.
//
// Never widens: keyMode is the narrowest mode the process can still read itself
// through, so "set it to exactly this whenever it differs" cannot loosen anything.
func enforceKeyMode(keyPath string) error {
	info, err := os.Stat(keyPath)
	if err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: %s: %w", keyPath, err)
	}
	if info.Mode().Perm() == keyMode {
		return nil
	}
	if err := os.Chmod(keyPath, keyMode); err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: %s is mode %04o and could not be narrowed to %04o (%w): a private key readable by "+
			"anyone but its owner is what docs/rules/security.md §7 is about", keyPath, info.Mode().Perm(), keyMode, err)
	}
	return nil
}

// needsRenewal reports whether a certificate is expired, or close enough to expiry
// that this process should not hand it to a browser.
//
// It is deliberately one-sided: it says nothing about a certificate being too *new*,
// too long-lived, or wrong in any other way. Every other ground for replacement is
// one this package refuses to act on, because acting on it would un-trust an
// operator over a matter of taste rather than a matter of the console working.
func needsRenewal(leaf *x509.Certificate, required []string, now time.Time) bool {
	if leaf == nil {
		return false
	}
	// **Too long is as unusable as too short.**
	//
	// A deployment can be serving a certificate valid for ten years, issued under
	// a longer default, before Apple's refusal of anything over 825 days (even for
	// a leaf trusted as its own anchor) was taken into account. Lowering the
	// constant changes nothing there: never-overwrite is scoped to a *valid*
	// certificate, and a ten-year certificate is valid for another decade. So the
	// repair would not reach the machines that have the defect.
	//
	// A certificate outliving the ceiling is not a certificate with plenty of time
	// left; it is one an evaluator rejects outright, which is the same practical
	// state as an expired one and deserves the same answer. Written against
	// `MaxValidityDays` rather than a date, so any future lowering of the ceiling
	// migrates existing installs by itself instead of needing a hand on each box.
	if leaf.NotAfter.After(now.Add(MaxValidityDays * 24 * time.Hour)) {
		return true
	}
	// **A name the console is dialled by and does not carry is the third way to be
	// unusable.**
	//
	// The names derived from the machine are what a container can see, and a
	// container cannot see the Docker host's LAN address — which, once the console
	// is published there, is the only address anybody dials. An install can
	// therefore hold a certificate that is perfectly valid and wrong, and
	// never-overwrite is scoped to a *valid* certificate: exactly the shape that
	// would leave a ten-year certificate on a deployment with no way to lower it.
	// Coverage, not equality, so extra names cost nothing and a
	// changed lease does not rotate a fingerprint an operator has accepted.
	if len(missingNames(leaf, required)) > 0 {
		return true
	}
	return now.After(leaf.NotAfter.Add(-RenewBeforeDays * 24 * time.Hour))
}

// missingNames returns the declared names this leaf would fail a dial for.
//
// The matcher is x509's own, not a comparison written here. That is the rule
// `docs/rules/testing.md` states as *derive the stand-in from what it stands in
// for*: VerifyHostname is what the client on the other end of the handshake runs,
// so a re-implementation could only ever be a second, drifting copy of it — and
// this is a package where the copy being subtly narrower produces a console that
// starts, reports success, and cannot be reached.
func missingNames(leaf *x509.Certificate, required []string) []string {
	if leaf == nil {
		return nil
	}
	dns, ips, err := splitNames(required)
	if err != nil {
		// A caller that validated first cannot reach this. One that did not gets
		// "everything is missing", which regenerates rather than silently serving
		// a certificate for names nobody checked.
		return required
	}
	var missing []string
	for _, n := range dns {
		if leaf.VerifyHostname(n) != nil {
			missing = append(missing, n)
		}
	}
	for _, ip := range ips {
		if leaf.VerifyHostname(ip.String()) != nil {
			missing = append(missing, ip.String())
		}
	}
	return missing
}

// renew replaces an expiring pair in place.
//
// Both temporary files are created and given their modes BEFORE either rename, so
// the failures that actually happen — no space, no permission, a read-only mount —
// happen while the old pair is still intact and still being served. The two renames
// are then adjacent and on the same filesystem, which is where rename cannot
// meaningfully fail.
//
// The window between the two renames is real and is documented rather than papered
// over: for those microseconds the certificate on disk does not match the key on
// disk. Nothing reads these files but this process at startup, so nothing observes
// it — but a future reader that does would see a pair that fails to load, and the
// refusal it gets is the correct answer to that state.
func renew(certPath, keyPath string, required []string, now time.Time) (tls.Certificate, error) {
	cert, err := generate(required, now)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}
	certPEM, keyPEM, err := encode(cert)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}

	certTmp, err := stage(certPath, certPEM, certMode)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}
	keyTmp, err := stage(keyPath, keyPEM, keyMode)
	if err != nil {
		_ = os.Remove(certTmp)
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}

	if err := os.Rename(certTmp, certPath); err != nil {
		_ = os.Remove(certTmp)
		_ = os.Remove(keyTmp)
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: replacing the expiring certificate at %s: %w", certPath, err)
	}
	if err := os.Rename(keyTmp, keyPath); err != nil {
		_ = os.Remove(keyTmp)
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf(
			"tlsca: the renewed certificate was written to %s but its key could not replace %s (%w): "+
				"the pair on disk no longer matches and the next start will refuse it. Remove both to generate a new pair",
			certPath, keyPath, err)
	}
	return cert, nil
}

// stage writes data to a temporary file beside path and returns its name.
//
// The mode is set with an explicit chmod for the same reason createExclusive does
// it, and the reason is not theoretical: without that chmod this package's suite
// would still pass under an ordinary umask, because a `go test` run inherits umask
// 022 and 022 subtracts nothing from either mode this package uses. Under the umask 077 that
// deploy/first-boot.sh generates under, os.CreateTemp's own 0600 would survive the
// rename and leave the operator unable to read the certificate whose fingerprint
// they were told to compare. TestRenewedModesSurviveAHostileUmask is the lock.
func stage(path string, data []byte, mode fs.FileMode) (string, error) {
	dir, base := filepath.Dir(path), filepath.Base(path)
	f, err := os.CreateTemp(dir, base+".renewing-*")
	if err != nil {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("tlsca: staging a replacement for %s: %w", path, err)
	}
	name := f.Name()
	fail := func(err error) (string, error) {
		f.Close()
		_ = os.Remove(name)
		return "", fmt.Errorf("tlsca: staging a replacement for %s: %w", path, err)
	}
	if _, err := f.Write(data); err != nil {
		return fail(err)
	}
	if err := f.Close(); err != nil {
		return fail(err)
	}
	if err := os.Chmod(name, mode); err != nil {
		_ = os.Remove(name)
		return "", fmt.Errorf("tlsca: setting mode %04o on the replacement for %s: %w", mode, path, err)
	}
	return name, nil
}

// encode renders a generated pair as the two PEM documents that go on disk.
func encode(cert tls.Certificate) (certPEM, keyPEM []byte, err error) {
	keyDER, err := x509.MarshalPKCS8PrivateKey(cert.PrivateKey)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, nil, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: encoding the generated key: %w", err)
	}
	return pem.EncodeToMemory(&pem.Block{Type: "CERTIFICATE", Bytes: cert.Certificate[0]}),
		pem.EncodeToMemory(&pem.Block{Type: "PRIVATE KEY", Bytes: keyDER}), nil
}

// generateTo makes a certificate and writes it, creating the directories.
//
// The certificate is written first and the key second, and a failure on the second
// removes the first: a half-written pair would be refused for the rest of the
// install's life by the partial-state branch above, which is the correct behaviour
// for a pair somebody else made and a silly one for a pair this call made itself.
func generateTo(certPath, keyPath string, required []string, now time.Time) (tls.Certificate, error) {
	for _, dir := range []string{filepath.Dir(certPath), filepath.Dir(keyPath)} {
		if err := os.MkdirAll(dir, dirMode); err != nil {
			return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: creating %s to hold the console certificate: %w", dir, err)
		}
	}

	cert, err := generate(required, now)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}

	certPEM, keyPEM, err := encode(cert)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}

	if err := createExclusive(certPath, certPEM, certMode); err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}
	if err := createExclusive(keyPath, keyPEM, keyMode); err != nil {
		// Undo this call's own certificate. Anything already on disk when this
		// call started was never reached — that is the refusal branch above.
		_ = os.Remove(certPath)
		return tls.Certificate{}, err
	}
	return cert, nil
}

// createExclusive writes data at path, refusing to touch an existing file.
//
// O_EXCL rather than a stat: the never-overwrite guarantee is then the kernel's and
// holds against a second process that started at the same moment, where a stat
// would have been a window. The stat earlier in EnsureSelfSigned is for the message
// it can write, not for the promise.
//
// The mode is set explicitly after creation because the process umask subtracts
// from the mode passed to open, and a umask of 077 would otherwise turn a
// certificate the operator has to read into one only this process can.
func createExclusive(path string, data []byte, mode fs.FileMode) error {
	f, err := os.OpenFile(path, os.O_WRONLY|os.O_CREATE|os.O_EXCL, mode)
	if err != nil {
		if errors.Is(err, fs.ErrExist) {
			return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: %s appeared while this one was being generated — another process is starting "+
				"against the same directory. Nothing was overwritten; start one process and retry", path)
		}
		return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: writing %s: %w", path, err)
	}
	if _, err := f.Write(data); err != nil {
		f.Close()
		_ = os.Remove(path)
		return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: writing %s: %w", path, err)
	}
	if err := f.Close(); err != nil {
		_ = os.Remove(path)
		return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: writing %s: %w", path, err)
	}
	if err := os.Chmod(path, mode); err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("tlsca: setting mode %04o on %s: %w", mode, path, err)
	}
	return nil
}

// generate makes the certificate itself. Every field is derived; nothing is asked.
func generate(required []string, now time.Time) (tls.Certificate, error) {
	key, err := ecdsa.GenerateKey(elliptic.P256(), rand.Reader)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: generating a P-256 key: %w", err)
	}

	serial, err := rand.Int(rand.Reader, new(big.Int).Lsh(big.NewInt(1), 128))
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: generating a serial number: %w", err)
	}

	dnsNames, ips := consoleNames(required)
	notBefore := now.Add(-backdate).Truncate(time.Second)

	template := &x509.Certificate{
		SerialNumber: serial,
		Subject: pkix.Name{
			// Ignored by every browser — SANs are what is checked — but it is what
			// a keychain full of certificates shows in its list, so it says which
			// machine this is rather than "localhost".
			CommonName:   dnsNames[0],
			Organization: []string{"Foyer management console"},
		},
		NotBefore: notBefore,
		// An exact duration, NOT AddDate. AddDate walks the calendar in the time's
		// own location, so a span crossing a DST transition is 825 days plus or
		// minus an hour — and this package issues at exactly the ceiling, where an
		// extra hour is what a "825 days or fewer" check may round into 826. A
		// Duration cannot drift.
		NotAfter: notBefore.Add(MaxValidityDays * 24 * time.Hour),
		// digitalSignature and nothing else: an ECDSA key never performs key
		// encipherment, so claiming it would widen what this certificate says it
		// is for without enabling anything.
		KeyUsage:              x509.KeyUsageDigitalSignature,
		ExtKeyUsage:           []x509.ExtKeyUsage{x509.ExtKeyUsageServerAuth},
		BasicConstraintsValid: true,
		IsCA:                  false,
		DNSNames:              dnsNames,
		IPAddresses:           ips,
	}

	der, err := x509.CreateCertificate(rand.Reader, template, template, &key.PublicKey, key)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: creating the certificate: %w", err)
	}
	leaf, err := x509.ParseCertificate(der)
	if err != nil {
		return tls.Certificate{}, fmt.Errorf("tlsca: re-reading the certificate just created: %w", err)
	}
	return tls.Certificate{Certificate: [][]byte{der}, PrivateKey: key, Leaf: leaf}, nil
}

// consoleNames derives every name a browser might have dialled to reach this
// console. See the package doc for the one it cannot derive.
//
// Loopback first and always: it is the documented access path (an SSH tunnel to
// 127.0.0.1) and the only one that is true regardless of where this runs.
func consoleNames(required []string) ([]string, []net.IP) {
	dnsNames := []string{"localhost"}
	seenDNS := map[string]bool{"localhost": true}
	add := func(n string) {
		if n != "" && !seenDNS[n] {
			seenDNS[n] = true
			dnsNames = append(dnsNames, n)
		}
	}
	if host, err := os.Hostname(); err == nil {
		for _, form := range hostnameForms(host) {
			add(form)
		}
	}

	ips := []net.IP{net.IPv4(127, 0, 0, 1), net.IPv6loopback}
	seenIP := map[string]bool{"127.0.0.1": true, "::1": true}
	var extra []net.IP
	addrs, err := net.InterfaceAddrs()
	if err == nil {
		for _, a := range addrs {
			n, ok := a.(*net.IPNet)
			if !ok {
				continue
			}
			// IsGlobalUnicast excludes loopback (already present), link-local —
			// which carries a zone a certificate cannot express — multicast and
			// the unspecified address. It keeps RFC 1918 and unique-local, which
			// is what a console on a venue LAN actually answers on.
			if !n.IP.IsGlobalUnicast() {
				continue
			}
			if seenIP[n.IP.String()] {
				continue
			}
			seenIP[n.IP.String()] = true
			extra = append(extra, n.IP)
		}
	}
	sort.Slice(extra, func(i, j int) bool { return extra[i].String() < extra[j].String() })

	// The declared names go on last and are NOT sorted with the derived ones:
	// they are the names this console was told it answers to, and a certificate
	// listing them in the order the deployment gave them is one an operator can
	// read against their own configuration.
	reqDNS, reqIPs, err := splitNames(required)
	if err != nil {
		// Unreachable: every caller parses first. Dropping them silently would be
		// the failure this package exists to avoid, so prefer no certificate.
		panic(err)
	}
	for _, n := range reqDNS {
		add(n)
	}
	for _, ip := range reqIPs {
		if seenIP[ip.String()] {
			continue
		}
		seenIP[ip.String()] = true
		extra = append(extra, ip)
	}
	return dnsNames, append(ips, extra...)
}

// splitNames turns the deployment's declared names into the two SAN fields, and
// refuses anything that is neither an address nor a name.
//
// **The split is the whole point and it is not cosmetic.** A verifier matching an
// IP the browser dialled looks at iPAddress SANs and nothing else, so "192.0.2.7"
// written into dNSName satisfies no dial at all — the certificate would list the
// address the operator typed, in a field no client consults, and the console would
// fail name validation while appearing to be configured correctly.
func splitNames(required []string) ([]string, []net.IP, error) {
	var dns []string
	var ips []net.IP
	for _, raw := range required {
		n := strings.ToLower(strings.TrimSuffix(strings.TrimSpace(raw), "."))
		if n == "" {
			continue
		}
		if ip := net.ParseIP(n); ip != nil {
			ips = append(ips, ip)
			continue
		}
		if !plausibleDNSName(n) {
			return nil, nil, fmt.Errorf(
				"tlsca: %q is neither an IP address nor a DNS name, so it cannot become a subjectAltName: "+
					"the console would start and then fail name validation for it", raw)
		}
		dns = append(dns, n)
	}
	return dns, ips, nil
}

// hostnameForms turns os.Hostname into the names a browser might carry.
//
// The full name, its first label (a compose service is reached as "foyer", not as
// the FQDN), and that label under .local, because Bonjour is how an operator's
// laptop reaches a small box on a venue LAN. A SAN for a name the machine does not
// answer to costs nothing — a certificate is only ever checked against the name
// that was dialled.
func hostnameForms(host string) []string {
	host = strings.ToLower(strings.TrimSuffix(strings.TrimSpace(host), "."))
	if host == "" || net.ParseIP(host) != nil || !plausibleDNSName(host) {
		return nil
	}
	out := []string{host}
	label, _, _ := strings.Cut(host, ".")
	if label != host {
		out = append(out, label)
	}
	if !strings.HasSuffix(host, ".local") {
		out = append(out, label+".local")
	}
	return out
}

// plausibleDNSName keeps a container's hostname from becoming a SAN no verifier
// would ever match — and keeps anything stranger out of a certificate entirely.
func plausibleDNSName(host string) bool {
	if len(host) > 253 {
		return false
	}
	for _, label := range strings.Split(host, ".") {
		if len(label) == 0 || len(label) > 63 {
			return false
		}
		if label[0] == '-' || label[len(label)-1] == '-' {
			return false
		}
		for _, r := range label {
			if (r < 'a' || r > 'z') && (r < '0' || r > '9') && r != '-' {
				return false
			}
		}
	}
	return true
}
